// Narrow-typed iterator reductions (array-ops arc): `xs.iter().take(k).sum()` on a narrow-width list
// must return that width's type and WRAP at its width — so it agrees with `xs.sum()`. Before this arc
// `Iterator<iN>.sum()` was typed `Unknown` and folded unmasked at 64 bits; now the reduction is typed
// at the element width and the generic adapter fold masks the total back to that width (traced through
// the width-preserving adapters `take`/`drop`/`chain`/`filter`). A directly list-backed
// `xs.iter().sum()` already delegates to the same buffer-folding kernel `xs.sum()` uses.
// expect: stdout "-294967296"
// expect: stdout "-294967296"
// expect: stdout "3"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: exit 0

// take(2) sums two 2e9 i32 values → 4e9, which wraps at i32 to -294967296.
tri = [2000000000i32, 2000000000i32, 2000000000i32]
echo tri.iter().take(2).sum()            // -294967296  (wraps at i32)

// The reference: summing the same two elements as a list wraps identically — no divergence between
// the iterator-adapter fold and the buffer fold.
pair = [2000000000i32, 2000000000i32]
echo pair.sum()                          // -294967296

// A non-overflowing narrow reduction stays exact.
small = [1i32, 2i32, 3i32]
echo small.iter().take(2).sum()          // 3

// The adapter fold agrees with the list reduction on the same window.
echo (tri.iter().take(2).sum() == pair.sum())  // true
